    "lawCode": "Amd §§28-104.2, 28-105.5 & 28-105.12.7.1, NYC Ad Cd",
    "lawSection": "New York City Administrative Code",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S4519\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the administrative code of the city of New York, in\nrelation to requiring notice to adjoining owners of construction,\nexcavation or demolition work\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\n\nThis bill would require notice to owners of adjoining property before\nconstruction, excavation or demolition work is performed.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1. Section 28-104.2: requires the New York City Department of\nBuildings (NYC DOB) to provide written notification to adjoining\nproperty owners when an application for approval of construction\ndocuments and when an application for a permit is submitted to the\nDepartment;\n\nSection 28-105.12.7.1 adds excavation to the list of construction work\nthat requires a person who obtains a permit for such work to procure\nand maintain insurance coverage for adjacent properties; and adds\nexcavation to the list of construction work that requires proof of\ninsurance coverage for adjacent properties to be submitted to the New\nYork City Department of Buildings when applying for a permit;\n\nSection 28-105.12.7.1 requires NYC DOB to provide proof of such\ninsurance and a copy of the permit to owners of adjoining property\nthirty days before construction, excavation or demolition work begins\nexcept in the event that emergency work is authorized as defined in\nsection 28-105.4.1 of this code; and requires, if such emergency work\nis necessary, the permit and proof of insurance to be provided to\nowners of adjoining property within a reasonable timeframe.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nConstruction, demolition, or excavation work performed on a building\nthat shares a wall with a neighboring building, is always complicated.\nTherefore the consequences of performing such work, especially as it\nrelates to threats to public safety, must be considered before the\nwork is performed. Particularly, in some instances, the neighbor may\nbe able to provide information which can highlight some potentially\ndangerous conditions.\n\nFor example, in June 2009 when a building collapsed on 493 Myrtle\nAvenue, in Brooklyn, New York, it collapsed largely because the\nengineers and contractors were unaware of several significant story\nvertical cracks, although they were visible to the tenants. As a\nresult, the appropriate precautions were not followed, and 493 Myrtle\ncollapsed to the ground. Several people were injured and fourteen were\nleft homeless. Thankfully, no one lost their lives. However, had this\nlegislation been in place, this unfortunate incident would not have\noccurred. Therefore, it is imperative that this legislature acts now,\nin order to avoid these accidents occurring in the future.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\n2013-14: A.3722\n2011-12: A.317-A\n2009-10: A.10511-B\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone to the State.\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
